[{"body":"\u003c!-- ccr-overview-v2 --\u003e\n\n### 🟢 Approval recommended\n\nThe webhook receipt ownership change has no active review issues.\n\n**No open issues** 🎉\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWhat changed in this PR\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nThis PR protects recovered Stripe webhook receipts from late completion or failure by an older handler, while leaving existing payment transaction guards unchanged.\n\n**Changes:**\n- Adds a database-generated claim UUID, renewed on each stale-claim takeover.\n- Requires the event ID, owned claim token, and processing status for completion and release.\n- Adds PostgreSQL regressions and updates unit-test expectations and architecture documentation.\n\n| File | Description |\r\n| ---- | ----------- |\r\n| `src/​server/​http/​stripe-webhook.web-handler.ts` | Renews claim tokens and carries ownership through completion and failure paths. |\r\n| `src/​server/​http/​stripe-webhook.web-handler.spec.ts` | Verifies token-aware receipt SQL and parameters. |\r\n| `src/​server/​http/​stripe-webhook-claim.postgres.spec.ts` | Tests late outcomes and replacement claims at identical timestamps. |\r\n| `src/​db/​schema/​stripe-webhook-events.ts` | Adds non-null `claim_id` with a database UUID default. |\r\n| `ARCHITECTURE.md` | Documents receipt ownership protection and its boundaries. |\r\n| `.changeset/​webhook-claim-ownership.md` | Records the webhook ownership fix as a patch release. |\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n\u003csub\u003eReview effort: Balanced\u003c/sub\u003e","url":null},{"body":"Thanks Lukas, this closes the B1 gap from the hackathon review cleanly. I walked the claim lifecycle at `914f2d9b` and found no blocker.\n\n**Checked and agreed**\n- Release and mark-processed now require `stripe_event_id + claim_id + status = 'processing'`, and takeover renews the token in SQL. So a late former owner can neither delete nor acknowledge a recovered claim, and a completed receipt can't be deleted, even by its own owner.\n- Two concurrent takeovers can't both win: the conditional UPDATE re-checks `processed_at \u003c= staleBefore` after the row lock, so the second one falls through to `duplicate-processing`.\n- The handler carries the token it actually won (insert or takeover) into both the failure path and `finalizeClaim`.\n- The three Postgres cases fail with main's event-only predicates (late failure, late success, same-timestamp replacement), so they prove the fence, including the status predicate.\n- `uuid NOT NULL DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()` passes the expand policy, and the seeded e2e rows rely on the default.\n\n**One change I'd suggest**\nA lost fence is silent. `markWebhookEventProcessed` and `releaseWebhookEventClaim` drop the affected-row count, so a fenced-out owner returns its normal response and logs nothing. That's the moment worth seeing: a handler outlived the 5-minute lease while another worker ran the same side effects. Suggestion: return `'owned' | 'lost'` via `.returning(...)` and `logWarning` with `eventId`/`claimId` on `'lost'`, keeping the HTTP response as is. The Postgres spec could then assert `'lost'` directly, and a concurrent-takeover case would be a cheap extra.\n\n**Two questions (pre-existing, fine outside this PR)**\n1. After a lost receipt (crash between the business commit and mark-processed), an add-on Checkout replay hits `state-conflict` in `validateCheckoutSessionBinding` and returns 409, although `completePaidAddonPurchaseCheckout` handles `already_applied`. So the takeover replay releases, and Stripe retries until it gives up. I ","url":null},{"body":"\u003c!-- ccr-overview-v2 --\u003e\n\n### 🟢 Approval recommended\n\nNo active review issues are identified for the Stripe webhook claim-ownership changes.\n\n**No open issues** 🎉\n\n\u003csub\u003eReview effort: Balanced\u003c/sub\u003e","url":null},{"body":"\u003c!-- qodo:billing-blocked --\u003e\n\n**ⓘ Qodo reviews are paused because the subscription is no longer a